#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261490 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bee6d4bf-79da-5eb3-a214-8c045dbce5bd" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Velocity of a Particle at the Moment When Its Acceleration is Zero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a34caa26-5f27-577f-8e03-422281cd8826" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261492}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a34caa26-5f27-577f-8e03-422281cd8826" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261492} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c45fb31-4247-5b73-97e3-1ea727fd41cd" :answer-field/key "time-s" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261491} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c97d4513-2be1-5a3e-adfd-fc5458c9499b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1/e^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261494}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c97d4513-2be1-5a3e-adfd-fc5458c9499b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1/e^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261494} :answer-field/id #uuid "86c23dbb-d50f-502d-9f65-2aa430c4048d" :answer-field/key "velocity-m-per-s" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261493}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9016b9c4-555f-5f54-8e92-6ab14881289d" :question/math-academy-id "q-73243" :question/problem "A particle $P$ is traveling along the $x$-axis with displacement $s(t)$ from a fixed origin $O$ given by\n\n$$\ns(t)=t{e}^{-t},\\,t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nFind the time at which the acceleration is zero, and find the value of the velocity at this moment.\n\nTime s: {{time-s}}\n\nVelocity m per s: {{velocity-m-per-s}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate $v(t)$ by differentiating $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t{e}^{-t}) \\\\  & =(1-t){e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we differentiate $v(t)$ to find $a(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}((1-t){e}^{-t}) \\\\  & =(t-2){e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we put $a=0$ to find the time when the acceleration is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =(t-2){e}^{-t} \\\\ t-2 & =0 \\\\ t & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the acceleration is zero when $t=2.$ To find the velocity at this moment, we substitute this back into $v(t)$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(2) & =(1-(2)){e}^{-(2)} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
